= Nationalist Movement Party

Born from an affliction of the veneration of violence, the glorification of power and the paranoia of change, the MHP is the embodiment of reaction. It is a cult of authority where its followers only abide and respect brute force, and feel a barbarous desire to submit to the supreme leader, the Generalissimo or the “Başbuğ”. This is the sentimentality that gave rise to the ultranationalist party known as the MHP, and today reminds us of the primitive face of humanity as the pinnacle of tribalism and primal instincts.

The 1960’s saw the rise of Socialist politics, both in the form of the electoral successes of TİP and the increased presence of Socialist ideals in the intelligentsia and mainstream politics. However, this shift also gave birth to a rabid reaction born out of the fear of radical change and the dismantling of social hierarchies. The party embodying this sentiment, MHP, was founded in 1969 as a radicalized successor of the more moderate right wing nationalist CKMP. The revitalized party blended Turkish Nationalism and Islamism into a new form of nationalism known as the “Turkic-Islamic Synthesis” or “Idealism” which defined Turkicness along the lines of Islam adherence to the conservative cultural norms. The party also boasts a paramilitary organization known as the “Grey Wolves” who strictly follow the fundamentalist party line and serve as the loyal lackeys who do the dirty work of assaulting and murdering the students and intellectuals who dare sympathize with Socialist ideals. Prior to the 12 March memorandum, tens of thousands of these so-called “commandos” had been trained in rural Anatolia by nationalist figures such as Muzaffer Özdağ and Alparslan Türkeş, ready to beat what they cant through words with bullets.

The leader of these men is Alparslan Türkeş, a self-proclaimed “strong-man” with the sole power to distinguish the right and wrong for his movement and the will to realize the dreams of an “uncorrupted” Turkey. Originally known as the Colonel who announced the 1960 coup against Adnan Menderes in the state radio, TRT, Türkeş had been part of the radical branch of the junta which opposed the reversal to democracy and desired the establishment of an authoritarian state. After the victory of the moderates in the junta, Türkeş was de-facto exiled to India and warded off from political power. However he returned to Turkey after the restrictions eased and got elected as the leader of CKMP in 1965, intent on transforming Turkish nationalism into a cult of authority and tradition. He used to pound the podiums with his sullen voice and proclaimed for the elimination of “foreign influences” which were corrupting the minds of the people, until the 12 March Memorandum put him and his party under scrutiny.

However, their recent silence should not relieve us, for it is a bandage for a wound that is still bleeding inside. Until the day our nation progresses enough to leave the nostalgia for the past and its traditions behind, Türkeş and his sycophants will keep proudly waving their banner of the three crescent, symbolizing “the three continents Turkicness dominated once: Europe, Africa and Asia"

- @root: Horst Wessel Lives On.
